## Explanation

A user wants to access `www.foobar.com`.

The browser resolves the domain name through DNS. The request is sent to the load balancer.

The load balancer, HAProxy, receives the incoming request and distributes it between Server 1 and Server 2.

Each server contains:
- Nginx as the web server
- An application server
- A copy of the application files
- A MySQL database

The database is configured as a Primary-Replica cluster.

The Primary database accepts write operations. The Replica database receives replicated data from the Primary and can be used for read operations.

## Why We Added Each Element

### Load Balancer

The load balancer distributes traffic between multiple servers.

It improves performance and helps avoid overloading a single server.

### Second Server

A second server increases capacity and provides redundancy.

If one server fails, the other server can still handle traffic.

### Second Web Server

Each server needs its own Nginx instance to receive and handle HTTP requests.

### Second Application Server

Each server needs an application server to execute the backend logic.

### Second Code Base

Each server needs a copy of the application files to run the website.

### Second Database

The second database is used as a Replica. It helps improve read performance and provides a copy of the data.

## Load Balancer Algorithm

The load balancer is configured with the Round Robin algorithm.

Round Robin sends requests to each server one after another.

Example:

Request 1 -> Server 1
Request 2 -> Server 2
Request 3 -> Server 1
Request 4 -> Server 2

## Active-Active or Active-Passive

This setup is Active-Active because both servers are receiving traffic at the same time.

### Active-Active

In an Active-Active setup, all servers are active and handling traffic.

### Active-Passive

In an Active-Passive setup, one server handles traffic and another server waits as a backup. The passive server is used only if the active server fails.

## Primary-Replica Database Cluster

In a Primary-Replica database cluster, the Primary node receives write operations.

The Replica node copies data from the Primary node through replication.

The application writes data to the Primary database and can read data from the Replica database.

## Difference Between Primary and Replica

### Primary node

The Primary database accepts both read and write operations.

### Replica node

The Replica database is mainly used for read operations. It does not usually accept writes from the application.

## Issues With This Infrastructure

### SPOF

The load balancer is a Single Point of Failure.

If the load balancer goes down, users cannot reach the servers.

The Primary database is also a SPOF for write operations. If it fails, the application cannot write new data.

### Security Issues

There is no firewall, so the infrastructure is more exposed to attacks.

There is no HTTPS, so traffic is not encrypted.

Sensitive data could be intercepted.

### No Monitoring

There is no monitoring system.

If a server fails or becomes overloaded, the team may not notice immediately.